          ],
          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 141,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When you are sick or nauseous, your skin doesn't just lose its color\u2014it can also take on a distinct, sickly greenish-gray, or \"sallow\" tone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 415,
                "end_index": 591,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Part 1: Why We Turn Pale (The Physiology)\nThe primary reason you look pale (pallor) when sick is a process called **vasoconstriction**\u2014the narrowing of your blood vessels"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 595,
                "end_index": 826,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Body\u2019s Priorities (Shunting Blood):** When you are fighting off an infection, feeling intense pain, or experiencing nausea, your autonomic nervous system (specifically your \"fight-or-flight\" response) kicks into overdrive"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 828,
                "end_index": 922,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To keep you alive, your body prioritizes your vital organs (like your heart, brain, and lungs)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 924,
                "end_index": 1042,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It constricts the blood vessels in your skin and diverts (shunts) that warm, oxygen-rich blood inward toward your core"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1045,
                "end_index": 1220,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Loss of the \"Rosy Flush\":** Healthy skin gets its warm, pinkish undertone from the red, oxygenated hemoglobin flowing through the tiny capillaries just beneath the surface"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                5,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1222,
                "end_index": 1327,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When vasoconstriction drains that blood away, your skin loses its warmth and appears pale, white, or gray"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1329,
                "end_index": 1436,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Dehydration and Low Blood Pressure:** Sickness often comes with dehydration or a drop in blood pressure"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1438,
                "end_index": 1546,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Lower blood volume means less pressure to push blood to your extremities and face, compounding the pale look"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1786,
                "end_index": 1973,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The \"Yellow + Blue\" Effect:** \n  * **Yellow:** Everyone\u2019s skin naturally has yellow undertones, which come from melanin, carotenoids, and the collagen and fat tissues under the skin"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1978,
                "end_index": 2139,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Blue:** The veins running just beneath your skin appear blue or purple due to the way light scatters through tissue, and because deoxygenated blood is darker"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2143,
                "end_index": 2255,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* Normally, the bright red of oxygen-rich blood in your capillaries masks these underlying yellow and blue tones"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2257,
                "end_index": 2347,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "But when you are sick and vasoconstriction pulls that red blood away, the red mask is gone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2349,
                "end_index": 2459,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The yellow of your skin and the blue of your veins mix visually, resulting in a **greenish-gray, sallow tint**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2636,
                "end_index": 2744,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This sudden exit of red blood is what causes the classic \"green around the gills\" look right before vomiting"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2746,
                "end_index": 2926,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Jaundice and Cyanosis:** In more severe illnesses, particularly those affecting the liver or gallbladder, a yellowish compound called bilirubin builds up in the body (jaundice)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                7,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2928,
                "end_index": 3102,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "If a person also has low oxygen levels (cyanosis, which makes skin look blueish), the combination of yellow jaundice and blue cyanosis can create a literal greenish skin tone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3104,
                "end_index": 3233,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **\"Green Sickness\" (Chlorosis):** Historically, severe iron-deficiency anemia was actually called \"green sickness\" or chlorosis"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3235,
                "end_index": 3414,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because patients lacked red hemoglobin to color their skin, their natural yellow undertone and blue veins mixed so severely that they appeared to have a greenish-yellow complexion"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3422,
                "end_index": 3504,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Part 3: Evolution is Watching\nHumans are evolutionary masters at reading faces"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3506,
                "end_index": 3652,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Research has shown that the human brain's visual system is incredibly sensitive to the color of human faces\u2014more so than almost any other object"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3656,
                "end_index": 3811,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "From an evolutionary standpoint, being able to instantly spot when someone\u2019s face loses its warm, red flush and turns pale-green was a survival advantage"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3813,
                "end_index": 3963,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It allowed our ancestors to quickly identify who in the tribe was contagious, sick, or too weak to keep up, helping to keep the rest of the group safe"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
          "retrieval_metadata": null,
